BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Grim History of the Death Penalty in Canada
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T190000
UID:6a63df710e562e28009c3fae@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Death by hanging was a grim reality within the Canadian crimin
 al justice system until its abolition in 1976. But what did capital punish
 ment look like in Canada? In this session\, author Lorna Poplak will prese
 nt the history of the death penalty in Canada\, with a focus on the incons
 istent application of the death sentence\, the treatment of women\, wrongf
 ul conviction\, the difficult journey toward abolition\, and the last two 
 hangings at Toronto’s notorious Don Jail.\nInformation Collection Notice
 :\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Publi
 c Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20
 (d). The information will only be used for event and program registration\
 , delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your in
 formation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 41
 6-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Pape/Danforth\, Second floor program room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a63df710e562e28009c3fae
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Wildlife-friendly Fall Gardens
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261017T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261017T150000
UID:6a6a4869f213992f00c7f72b@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:At this time of year\, many people admire the fall colours but
  also feel the need to rake and dispose of leaves\, cut dead stems\, and r
 emove fallen branches. But what if this tidying up negatively affects our 
 gardens and pollinators? While we may not see the bees and butterflies dur
 ing the fall and winter\, they are still around!\nIn this presentation\, D
 orte Windmuller\, a Butterflyway Ranger of the David Suzuki Foundation\, w
 ill discuss how to maintain and protect wildlife habitat even throughout t
 he colder months\, and how “leave the leaves” can help nature come bac
 k stronger the next season.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal 
 information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under 
 the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The informa
 tion will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and e
 valuation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be
  directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789
  Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Room B1 (located in the basement)
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6a4869f213992f00c7f72b
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
