BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Pressed Flower Bracelet Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260810T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260810T150000
UID:6a209531e13d3b4603b65695@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Discover the elegant art of botanical preservation in this imm
 ersive\, hands-on crafting experience. In this 2-hour workshop\, you will 
 learn the fundamentals of flower pressing from selecting and harvesting t
 he best blooms to masterfully storing them. While exploring these timeless
  techniques\, you will design and create your own one-of-a-kind botanical 
 bangle cuff bracelet to take home.\nWhat You Will Learn:\nFlower Pressing 
 101:\nEssential techniques for perfectly preserving real botanical element
 s.\nHarvesting Secrets:\nHow to choose the right flowers and the optimal t
 imes to pick them.\nStorage Best Practices:\nProfessional methods to keep 
 your preserved flowers vibrant over time.\nJewelry Assembly:\nGuided steps
  to design and construct a wearable piece of art.\nWorkshop Details:\nSkil
 l Level:\nPerfect for beginners. No prior gardening or jewelry-making expe
 rience is required.\nAll-Inclusive Experience:\nYour registration covers a
  curated material kit to keep. Specialty tools are provided for use during
  class.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this 
 form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries
  Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be us
 ed for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions
  about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerl
 ine staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toro
 nto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Barbara Frum\, Located in Room B on the third floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a209531e13d3b4603b65695
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Memory & Aging: What's Normal\, What's Not\, and What You Can Do A
 bout It
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T150000
UID:6a35da78296ea565de2d7b6b@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Psychologist Nicole Anderson discusses how different kinds of 
 cognitive functions change as a part of healthy aging and provides example
 s of normal memory slips and not-so-normal memory slips. This presentation
  discusses factors affecting cognition\, including medical factors and eve
 ryday lifestyle factors\, then provides a number of memory strategies that
  people can use to improve their memory function.\nNicole Anderson is the 
 Senior Scientist at the Rotman Research Institute (Baycrest) and  Professo
 r\, Psychology and Psychiatry (University of Toronto). She is the author o
 f "Living with mild cognitive impairment: a guide to maximizing brain heal
 th and reducing risk of dementia"\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe per
 sonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library 
 under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The i
 nformation will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery
  and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information 
 can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981
 \, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall (Ground Floor)
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a35da78296ea565de2d7b6b
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
